.TH SINDOOK-SCAN 1 "2026-08-27" "sindook 0.9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sindook-scan \- audit TLS endpoints and local keys for weak cryptography
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sindook scan tls
[\fB\-json\fP] [\fB\-timeout\fP \fISECONDS\fP] \fIHOST\fP[:\fIPORT\fP]...
.br
.B sindook scan files
[\fB\-json\fP] [\fIPATH\fP...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reports on the cryptographic posture of network endpoints and local
files. Both modes are read-only: scan opens ordinary TLS connections or
reads files, and never sends exploit payloads, guesses credentials, or
captures traffic.
.PP
.B scan tls
connects to each host (port 443 unless given) and reports certificate
expiry, certificate key strength, chain and hostname validity, the
negotiated protocol version, whether the server still accepts TLS 1.0
or 1.1 (deprecated by RFC 8996), and whether the server supports a
hybrid post-quantum key exchange (X25519MLKEM768, SecP256r1MLKEM768,
or SecP384r1MLKEM1024). Recorded traffic from a server without hybrid
key exchange may be decryptable by a future quantum computer, the
harvest-now, decrypt-later problem. A probe that cannot reach a
conclusion (timeout, interference, or a restricted local runtime)
reports itself as inconclusive instead of guessing. Ports that
upgrade with STARTTLS (such as 25 and 587) are not supported; scan
implicit-TLS ports.
.PP
.B scan files
walks the given paths (default: the current directory) and reports
private keys stored without a passphrase, key files with permissive
file modes (best effort, platform dependent), expired or soon-expiring
certificates, and weak key sizes. Candidates are found by common file
names. It recognizes PEM, OpenSSH, and PuTTY key files and DER
certificates; PKCS#12 containers are reported but not opened.
.PP
Findings use the same shape as
.BR sindook-doctor (1):
a status (ok, warning, or error), a detail, and a remediation. The
exit code is 0 when nothing is an error, 1 otherwise, so the command
works in cron jobs and CI.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-json
Print one machine-readable JSON report to stdout instead of text.
.TP
.B \-timeout \fISECONDS\fP
Per-connection timeout for tls scans. Default 10.
.SH SCOPE
scan answers one question: which of my endpoints and key files rely on
cryptography that is expiring, misconfigured, or quantum-vulnerable.
For an exhaustive cipher-suite and TLS-implementation audit, use a
dedicated tool such as testssl.sh alongside it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Audit two endpoints, one on a non-default port:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sindook scan tls example.com mail.example.com:993
.RE
.fi
.PP
Audit SSH and TLS key material on a server:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sindook scan files ~/.ssh /etc/ssl/private
.RE
.fi
.PP
Fail a CI job when an endpoint has an expired certificate:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sindook scan tls -json api.example.com | jq '.errors'
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sindook (1),
.BR sindook-doctor (1),
.BR sindook-seal (1)
